      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The bias, RMSE, spatial correlation and Figure 1 must be computed only where model and WOA23 both have data, with all fields on one grid under one common mask.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Regrid all three models and WOA23 to the 1° grid, build one mask over all of them with `build_comparison_mask` and apply `.where(mask)` to every field before the bias map, RMSE and spatial correlation; per-pair masks would score each model on a different piece of ocean.",
        "`ocean_only=True` (the default) is correct here because WOA23 is part of the comparison; report the mask's cell count beside the metrics — a mask much smaller than the fields means the products disagree about where the ocean is."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "historical-ssp245-member-continuity",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/historical-ssp245-member-continuity.md",
      "sha256": "02341c56015c6296db14585b4561fc32ca6b4e52e2264bec50eee6746c1d55f6",
      "reason": "The requested ensemble-member caveat applies because CESM2's local ssp245 and historical runs use different members, so historical-to-ssp245 differences mix forced change with internal variability.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "CESM2's local historical is r1i1p1f1 but its ssp245 is r10i1p1f1 (historical r10 is not in the archive): its delta SSS and its ssp245 slope contain forced change plus a difference in internal variability between two members — state this caveat; GFDL-ESM4 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR use r1 in both.",
        "Do not fit one trend across the 2014/2015 join for CESM2; each segment is fitted separately, as asked."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "The Atlantic, Indian and Pacific means depend on the chosen basin partition and must state how marginal seas and the separate Southern and Arctic basins are handled.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Basin statistics use the WOA23 basin mask (`select_basin`, `load_basin_mask`), not the OMIP 10-region Ofx `basin` (absent locally); state that marginal seas are folded into their parent ocean via `include_marginal_seas=True`.",
        "Named-ocean means use the surface labels applied through the whole column (`depth_dependent=False`); each level's own labels are for water-mass work."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-static-grid-fields",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-static-grid-fields.md",
      "sha256": "2ad0548ca0993fa57d8f1fad27a267dcf9c0e1d007a74343d4bbdbf5b4f1ca17",
      "reason": "The SSS level weighting and global means require areacello and volcello, whose definitions as tracer-cell horizontal area and cell volume constrain the calculation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Layer thickness respecting partial bottom cells is `volcello / areacello` (`cell_thickness_like`), not the `lev_bnds` difference, which is right only for full cells."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "Global surface SSS means should be area-weighted over surface ocean cells, while the per-column SSS level weighting uses full cell volumes rather than partial overlaps.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Global mean SSS is the area-weighted surface mean, Σ SSS·areacello / Σ areacello over valid cells; weights must be NaN-aware and restricted to the same cells as the data — never a plain `mean()` over (y, x).",
        "`compute_difference_metrics` weights by area by default; `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'` for the spatial pattern correlation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-so-practical-salinity",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-so-practical-salinity.md",
      "sha256": "96f2763290996040d675ca6e8f997ea29fa320b8f6e6793bf2c6fcb739345e19",
      "reason": "CMIP6 so is to be compared as Practical Salinity in psu, matching the requested units and the WOA23 comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Model `so` and WOA23 salinity are both practical salinity on the same scale: \"0.001\", \"1e-3\", \"1\" and \"psu\" all denote the same number — no conversion before differencing or labelling in psu.",
        "Only convert S_P to S_A before any TEOS-10/gsw density call."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "Trends and global means should be computed natively where possible, with regridding to the WOA23 or common longitude-latitude grid only for maps and pattern comparisons because regridding alters masks and conservation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute every number (annual global mean SSS, trends, basin means, delta SSS) on each model's own grid with its own `areacello`; regrid only for the bias map, RMSE and pattern correlation on the common mask, and state the method ('conservative' toward the coarser 1° grid).",
        "Put curvilinear model fields on a regular longitude–latitude grid for display only (Figures 1–2); the trend itself is computed before that display regridding."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The trend confidence intervals, R-squared interpretation and p < 0.05 stippling require accounting for strong autocorrelation and effective sample size.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Fit the linearly-detrended-free straight line explicitly with `method='linear'`; quote the effective-sample-size-corrected 95% interval and p-value (not the naive monthly one), call it significant only if the interval excludes zero, and give R².",
        "On the trend map, remove the seasonal cycle first (same trend, sharper test) and stipple cells significant at p<0.05 on top of the full slope field; for the spatial correlation with WOA23 read the coefficient, not its p-value."
      ]
